/-!
# Embedded resolution of surfaces

This file states the embedded resolution of surfaces of Cossart, Jannsen and Saito [CJS20]. The text is in Volume 4, Chapter 3 of the handbook (Definitions 3.1.1 to 3.1.3 and Theorem 3.1.4). The statement is a definition of a proposition. This file does not prove it.

## Main definitions

* `IsRegularSystemOfParameters`: a regular system of parameters of a local ring ([Mat89, Section 14]).
* `stalkIdeal`: the stalk of an ideal sheaf at a point, as an ideal of the local ring.
* `IsSncDivisor`: a divisor with simple normal crossings (node SG0000000V, Stacks tag 0BI9).
* `HasNormalCrossings`: a regular closed subscheme has normal crossings with a divisor (node SG0000000V).
* `IsPermissible`: a permissible center (node SG0000000T, [Hir64]).
* `IsRegularCodimOne`: a closed subscheme that is regular of codimension 1 at its points.
* `strictTransform`: the strict transform of a reduced closed subscheme.
* `IsEmbeddedResolution`: an embedded resolution (Definition 3.1.3, in a weaker form).
* `EmbeddedResolutionSurface`: Theorem 3.1.4 for a regular scheme of finite type over a field (node SG0000001C).

## The choices

All the local conditions are stated at the stalks. Mathlib has no stalk of an ideal sheaf, so `stalkIdeal I z` is the ideal of `𝒪_{Z,z}` generated by the germs of the sections of `I` on the affine opens that contain `z`.

Mathlib has no regular system of parameters. Here a regular system of parameters of a local ring `R` is a finite set of `dim R` elements that generates the maximal ideal. A Noetherian local ring with such a set is regular (`isRegularLocalRing_of_isRegularSystemOfParameters`), and each regular local ring has one (`exists_isRegularSystemOfParameters`).

Mathlib has no irreducible components of a divisor as subschemes. Thus `IsSncDivisor` follows Stacks tag 0BI9: at each point, the ideal of the divisor is generated by the product of some members of a regular system of parameters. For a divisor on a regular Noetherian scheme this is the same as Definition 3.1.2: the components are regular and meet transversally.

Mathlib has no excellent schemes and no blowups of schemes. Thus `EmbeddedResolutionSurface` asks for a scheme `Z` that is regular and of finite type over a field. This is the case that the project needs first. It does not ask that `π` is a sequence of blowups in permissible centers. It asks for a proper morphism `π` that is an isomorphism over the points where `X` is regular, with a simple normal crossings divisor `E` over the rest. The theorem of [CJS20] gives such a morphism.

## References

* [CJS20] V. Cossart, U. Jannsen and S. Saito, *Desingularization: invariants and strategy*, Lecture Notes in Mathematics 2270, 2020.
* [Hir64] H. Hironaka, *Resolution of singularities of an algebraic variety over a field of characteristic zero*, 1964.
* [Mat89] H. Matsumura, *Commutative ring theory*, 1989.
* [Sta] The Stacks project authors, *The Stacks project*.
-/

open AlgebraicGeometry CategoryTheory IsLocalRing TopologicalSpace TensorProduct

universe u

namespace Singularity

/-! ### Regular systems of parameters -/

section Local

variable {R : Type*} [CommRing R]

/-- A regular system of parameters of a local ring `R`: a finite set `s` of `dim R` elements that generates the maximal ideal ([Mat89, Section 14]). The members of `s` are in the maximal ideal. -/
def IsRegularSystemOfParameters [IsLocalRing R] (s : Finset R) : Prop :=
  Ideal.span (s : Set R) = maximalIdeal R ∧ (s.card : WithBot ℕ∞) = ringKrullDim R

/-- The ideal `I` of a local ring is the ideal of a divisor with simple normal crossings: `I` is generated by the product of a subset `t` of a regular system of parameters. If `t` is empty, then `I` is the unit ideal. -/
def IsSncAt [IsLocalRing R] (I : Ideal R) : Prop :=
  ∃ s : Finset R, IsRegularSystemOfParameters s ∧ ∃ t ⊆ s, I = Ideal.span {∏ x ∈ t, x}

/-- The ideal `J` of a regular subscheme has normal crossings with the ideal `I` of a divisor: there is a regular system of parameters `s` and two disjoint subsets `a` and `b` of `s` such that `a` generates `J` and the product of `b` generates `I`. -/
def HasNormalCrossingsAt [IsLocalRing R] (J I : Ideal R) : Prop :=
  ∃ s : Finset R, IsRegularSystemOfParameters s ∧ ∃ a ⊆ s, ∃ b ⊆ s, Disjoint a b ∧
    J = Ideal.span (a : Set R) ∧ I = Ideal.span {∏ x ∈ b, x}

/-- The quotient `R / Q` is normally flat along `P / Q`: for each `n`, the module `(P ^ n + Q) / (P ^ (n + 1) + Q)` is flat over `R / P`.

In `R / Q` the ideal `P / Q` has the powers `(P ^ n + Q) / Q`, so this module is `(P / Q) ^ n / (P / Q) ^ (n + 1)`. The ideal `P` kills it, so it is the module `(R / P) ⊗[R] M` that the definition uses. -/
def IsNormallyFlatAt (P Q : Ideal R) : Prop :=
  ∀ n : ℕ, Module.Flat (R ⧸ P)
    ((R ⧸ P) ⊗[R] (↥(P ^ n ⊔ Q) ⧸ Submodule.comap (P ^ n ⊔ Q).subtype (P ^ (n + 1) ⊔ Q)))

/-- A Noetherian local ring with a regular system of parameters is a regular local ring. -/
theorem isRegularLocalRing_of_isRegularSystemOfParameters [IsLocalRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R]
    {s : Finset R} (hs : IsRegularSystemOfParameters s) : IsRegularLocalRing R := by
  refine IsRegularLocalRing.of_spanFinrank_maximalIdeal_le R ?_
  rw [← hs.2, ← hs.1]
  exact_mod_cast (Submodule.spanFinrank_span_le_ncard_of_finite s.finite_toSet).trans_eq
    (Set.ncard_coe_finset s)

/-- A regular local ring has a regular system of parameters. -/
theorem exists_isRegularSystemOfParameters [IsRegularLocalRing R] :
    ∃ s : Finset R, IsRegularSystemOfParameters s := by
  obtain ⟨s, hcard, hspan⟩ :=
    Submodule.FG.exists_span_finset_card_eq_spanFinrank (maximalIdeal R).fg_of_isNoetherianRing
  exact ⟨s, hspan, by rw [hcard]; exact IsRegularLocalRing.spanFinrank_maximalIdeal⟩

/-- In a regular local ring the unit ideal has simple normal crossings: it is the empty divisor. -/
theorem isSncAt_top [IsRegularLocalRing R] : IsSncAt (⊤ : Ideal R) := by
  obtain ⟨s, hs⟩ := exists_isRegularSystemOfParameters (R := R)
  exact ⟨s, hs, ∅, Finset.empty_subset s, by simp⟩

end Local

/-! ### The stalk of an ideal sheaf -/

section Stalk

variable {Z : Scheme.{u}}

/-- The stalk at `z` of an ideal sheaf `I` on `Z`: the ideal of the local ring `𝒪_{Z,z}` generated by the germs at `z` of the sections of `I` on the affine opens that contain `z`. -/
noncomputable def stalkIdeal (I : Z.IdealSheafData) (z : Z) : Ideal (Z.presheaf.stalk z) :=
  ⨆ (U : Z.affineOpens) (h : z ∈ U.1), (I.ideal U).map (Z.presheaf.germ U.1 z h).hom

/-- Each point of a scheme is in an affine open. -/
lemma exists_affineOpens_mem (z : Z) : ∃ U : Z.affineOpens, z ∈ U.1 := by
  obtain ⟨_, ⟨U, hU, rfl⟩, hzU, -⟩ :=
    Z.isBasis_affineOpens.exists_subset_of_mem_open (Set.mem_univ z) isOpen_univ
  exact ⟨⟨U, hU⟩, hzU⟩

/-- The stalk of an ideal sheaf contains the germs of its sections on an affine open. -/
lemma map_ideal_le_stalkIdeal (I : Z.IdealSheafData) {z : Z} (U : Z.affineOpens) (h : z ∈ U.1) :
    (I.ideal U).map (Z.presheaf.germ U.1 z h).hom ≤ stalkIdeal I z :=
  le_iSup₂_of_le U h le_rfl

@[simp]
lemma stalkIdeal_top (z : Z) : stalkIdeal (⊤ : Z.IdealSheafData) z = ⊤ := by
  obtain ⟨U, hU⟩ := exists_affineOpens_mem z
  refine eq_top_iff.mpr (le_trans ?_ (map_ideal_le_stalkIdeal ⊤ U hU))
  simp [Scheme.IdealSheafData.ideal_top, Ideal.map_top]

@[simp]
lemma stalkIdeal_bot (z : Z) : stalkIdeal (⊥ : Z.IdealSheafData) z = ⊥ := by
  simp [stalkIdeal, Scheme.IdealSheafData.ideal_bot]

/-- At a point of the support, the stalk of an ideal sheaf is in the maximal ideal. -/
lemma stalkIdeal_le_maximalIdeal {I : Z.IdealSheafData} {z : Z} (hz : z ∈ I.support) :
    stalkIdeal I z ≤ maximalIdeal (Z.presheaf.stalk z) := by
  refine iSup₂_le fun U hzU => Ideal.map_le_iff_le_comap.mpr fun s hs => ?_
  have h := (Scheme.IdealSheafData.mem_support_iff_of_mem hzU).mp hz
  rw [Scheme.mem_zeroLocus_iff] at h
  have h' := h s hs
  rw [Scheme.mem_basicOpen _ _ _ hzU] at h'
  exact (IsLocalRing.mem_maximalIdeal _).mpr h'

/-- Outside the support, the stalk of an ideal sheaf is the unit ideal. -/
lemma stalkIdeal_eq_top_of_notMem {I : Z.IdealSheafData} {z : Z} (hz : z ∉ I.support) :
    stalkIdeal I z = ⊤ := by
  obtain ⟨U, hzU⟩ := exists_affineOpens_mem z
  rw [Scheme.IdealSheafData.mem_support_iff_of_mem hzU, Scheme.mem_zeroLocus_iff] at hz
  push Not at hz
  obtain ⟨s, hs, hzs⟩ := hz
  rw [Scheme.mem_basicOpen _ _ _ hzU] at hzs
  exact Ideal.eq_top_of_isUnit_mem _
    (map_ideal_le_stalkIdeal I U hzU (Ideal.mem_map_of_mem _ hs)) hzs

/-- A point is in the support of an ideal sheaf if and only if the stalk is not the unit ideal. -/
lemma stalkIdeal_ne_top_iff {I : Z.IdealSheafData} {z : Z} :
    stalkIdeal I z ≠ ⊤ ↔ z ∈ I.support :=
  ⟨fun h => by_contra fun hz => h (stalkIdeal_eq_top_of_notMem hz),
    fun hz => ne_top_of_le_ne_top (maximalIdeal.isMaximal _).ne_top (stalkIdeal_le_maximalIdeal hz)⟩

end Stalk

/-! ### Divisors, centers and the strict transform -/

section Scheme

variable {Z : Scheme.{u}}

/-- The ideal sheaf `D` on `Z` is a divisor with simple normal crossings (Definition 3.1.2, Stacks tag 0BI9): at each point `z`, the stalk of `D` is generated by the product of some members of a regular system of parameters of `𝒪_{Z,z}`.

At a point outside `D` the product is empty. The definition asks for a regular system of parameters at each point, so `Z` must be regular: if `Z` is locally Noetherian, then each local ring of `Z` is regular by `isRegularLocalRing_of_isRegularSystemOfParameters`. The members of a regular system of parameters are not zero divisors, so `D` is an effective Cartier divisor. The product has no repeated factor, so `D` is reduced. -/
def IsSncDivisor (D : Z.IdealSheafData) : Prop :=
  ∀ z : Z, IsSncAt (stalkIdeal D z)

/-- The closed subscheme `X` of `Z` has normal crossings with the divisor `D` (Definition 3.1.2): at each point `z` of `X`, there is a regular system of parameters of `𝒪_{Z,z}` such that some of its members generate the stalk of `X`, and the product of some other members generates the stalk of `D`.

This condition implies that `X` is regular at `z`, because the quotient of a regular local ring by members of a regular system of parameters is regular. -/
def HasNormalCrossings (X D : Z.IdealSheafData) : Prop :=
  ∀ z ∈ X.support, HasNormalCrossingsAt (stalkIdeal X z) (stalkIdeal D z)

/-- The closed subscheme `C` is a permissible center for the closed subscheme `X` of `Z` (Definition 3.1.1, node SG0000000T): `C` is contained in `X`, `C` is regular, and `X` is normally flat along `C`.

The conditions are stated at the stalks of `𝒪_Z`. At a point `z` of `C`, the local ring of `C` is `𝒪_{Z,z} / C_z` and the local ring of `X` is `𝒪_{Z,z} / X_z`. The definition asks that `𝒪_{Z,z} / C_z` is regular and that `𝒪_{Z,z} / X_z` is normally flat along `C_z / X_z` (`IsNormallyFlatAt`). Flatness of a quasi-coherent sheaf is a condition at the stalks, so this is the normal flatness of Definition 3.1.1. -/
def IsPermissible (X C : Z.IdealSheafData) : Prop :=
  X ≤ C ∧ ∀ z ∈ C.support, IsRegularLocalRing (Z.presheaf.stalk z ⧸ stalkIdeal C z) ∧
    IsNormallyFlatAt (stalkIdeal C z) (stalkIdeal X z)

/-- The closed subscheme `D` of `Z` is regular of codimension 1 at its points: at each point `z` of `D`, the ring `𝒪_{Z,z} / D_z` is a regular local ring of dimension `dim 𝒪_{Z,z} - 1`. -/
def IsRegularCodimOne (D : Z.IdealSheafData) : Prop :=
  ∀ z ∈ D.support, IsRegularLocalRing (Z.presheaf.stalk z ⧸ stalkIdeal D z) ∧
    ringKrullDim (Z.presheaf.stalk z ⧸ stalkIdeal D z) + 1 = ringKrullDim (Z.presheaf.stalk z)

/-- The strict transform of the closed subscheme `X` of `Z` under `π : Z' ⟶ Z`, with respect to an open `U` of `Z` over which `π` is an isomorphism: the reduced closed subscheme of `Z'` on the closure of `π⁻¹(X ∩ U)`.

For a reduced `X` this is the scheme-theoretic closure of `π⁻¹(X ∩ U)`. In `EmbeddedResolutionSurface` the strict transform must have normal crossings with a divisor, so it must be regular, and the reduced structure is the right one. -/
noncomputable def strictTransform {Z' : Scheme.{u}} (X : Z.IdealSheafData) (π : Z' ⟶ Z)
    (U : Z.Opens) : Z'.IdealSheafData :=
  Scheme.IdealSheafData.vanishingIdeal
    ⟨closure (π.base ⁻¹' ((X.support : Set Z) ∩ U)), isClosed_closure⟩

/-- The morphism `π : Z' ⟶ Z` is an embedded resolution of the closed subscheme `X` of `Z`, with the open `U` of `Z` and the exceptional divisor `E` on `Z'`. The conditions are these.

* `Z'` is regular.
* `π` is proper.
* Each point outside `U` is a point of `X` where `X` is not regular.
* `π` is an isomorphism over `U`.
* The support of `E` is the preimage of the complement of `U`.
* `E` is a divisor with simple normal crossings.
* The strict transform of `X` has normal crossings with `E`, so it is regular.

Definition 3.1.3 asks for a sequence of blowups in permissible centers. This structure does not, because Mathlib has no blowups of schemes. It keeps the properties of the result. The condition on the support of `E` has a consequence: each generic point of `Z'` is outside `E`, because its local ring is a field, so the preimage of `U` is dense in `Z'`. -/
structure IsEmbeddedResolution {Z' : Scheme.{u}} (X : Z.IdealSheafData) (π : Z' ⟶ Z)
    (U : Z.Opens) (E : Z'.IdealSheafData) : Prop where
  regular : IsRegularScheme Z'
  proper : IsProper π
  compl_subset : (U : Set Z)ᶜ ⊆ X.subschemeι.base '' (regularLocus X.subscheme)ᶜ
  isIso_over : IsIso (π ∣_ U)
  support_eq : (E.support : Set Z') = π.base ⁻¹' (U : Set Z)ᶜ
  snc : IsSncDivisor E
  normalCrossings : HasNormalCrossings (strictTransform X π U) E

end Scheme

/-! ### The theorem of Cossart, Jannsen and Saito -/

/-- Embedded resolution of surfaces (Theorem 3.1.4, [CJS20], node SG0000001C), for a regular scheme of finite type over a field: let `Z` be a regular scheme of finite type over a field `k`, and let `X` be a reduced closed subscheme of `Z` of dimension at most 2. Then `X` has an embedded resolution `π : Z' ⟶ Z` in the sense of `IsEmbeddedResolution`.

The field `k` is any field. It need not be perfect. The theorem of [CJS20] holds for each regular excellent scheme `Z`, and a scheme of finite type over a field is excellent. The theorem of [CJS20] also gives a canonical sequence of blowups in permissible centers. This statement asks only for the result. This file does not prove the statement. -/
def EmbeddedResolutionSurface : Prop :=
  ∀ (k : Type u) [Field k] (Z : Scheme.{u}) (f : Z ⟶ Spec (.of k)) [LocallyOfFiniteType f]
    [QuasiCompact f], IsRegularScheme Z → ∀ X : Z.IdealSheafData, IsReduced X.subscheme →
      topologicalKrullDim X.subscheme ≤ 2 →
        ∃ (Z' : Scheme.{u}) (π : Z' ⟶ Z) (U : Z.Opens) (E : Z'.IdealSheafData),
          IsEmbeddedResolution X π U E

end Singularity
